Z||000000||Philphine||04-07-2001||03:15 PM||||<BR>i've been having trouble with my '87 <BR>coupe once it warms up. sometimes at <BR>stops if i sit long enough it stalls <BR>(not always, but i'm never sure when it <BR>will). lately if i turn it off and it <BR>sits for a certain amount of time it's <BR>hard to start (not sure how long. a few minutes getting gas, it starts right up. go to work and come out to got home starts right up. go into a store for 20 -50 min., it's hard to start). yesterday was the worst. it stalled a few times as i was driving.<P>the only code i get is 35, but i changed the idle speed control about this time last year. how often should they normaly be changed? also at that time my coil pack, fuel filter, and i think plugs wires and air filter were changed. i was having similar problems last spring, it's almost a yearly thing.<P>i'm leaving out to get a 9volt battery and clip for the test recommended for the idle control. i guess i'll get an o2 sensor since it wasn't changed the last go round. my vaccume guage reads like always. the air fuel reads kind of low and once or twice stopped reading (or i was getting a mixture so rich it pegged at the top. guess i'll try to hook up a pressure tester to check my fuel pump too (got a guage but it's not hooked up yet. i'd have to just put a test guage in line and see were it sits when the key is switched on).<P>leaving out to pick up stuff and try working on it, but if anyone has any ideas i'd appreciate them.    ||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||04-07-2001||03:55 PM||||A couple of things come to mind...  The code 35 really means idle control and circuit.  Check the harness and make sure the computer can 'push' the steppers;  Also, how clean is your throttle body.  It may be that the steppers are turning but the expected airflow ain't happening so it sets the code.  I've noticed that the placement of the PCV intake and idle flow intake cause oil residue to build up in the throttle body when the PVC isn't working properly.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 04-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||04-07-2001||08:19 PM||||didn't get any further than changing the o2 sensor. needed to do something else close by so i drove it. but once it warmed up, same problem. dose the idle speed control have any bearing if your're at a driving speed? 'caise if it dosen't, i guess i need to be looking inanother direction.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-07-2001||11:33 PM||||Exactly what ahs been replaced....<P>check for vacuum leaks. That would cause IAC errors. <P>check the PCV line and it's rubber joints. clamp them (don't over tighten the clamps)<P>check all the other lines and the base of the TBI.<P>also some bad news. you may need to replace the fuel pump. <P>What you really need is a scanner so you can see what all the sensors are doing. You really can't work on a DIS motor w/o one.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||04-08-2001||01:02 AM||||I re-read your orignal post.  If the car is stalling while your driving it (foot on the pedal) it is not the idle circuit or airflow.  If it is a std and stalls during a shift could still be in the neighborhood.  Sounds like some kind of fule problem though.  Typically a hard start after warm & sitting is excess fuel.  Check to see if the fuel pressure leaks out of the injector.  This doesn't explain the stall when driving unless it is really leaky.  How's you cat?||||reg||1
Z||000005||GarethWright||04-08-2001||01:14 AM||||Scanner eh? Where might I get such a likely-to-be-expensive toy? And how expensive are they really?<BR>Thanks<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||04-08-2001||03:18 PM||||had guard today so i just got home. ogre, all i've replaced just recently is the o2 sensor yestrday (4/8). all the other changes were last spring when i had a similar problem. checked all my vacuum lines visually, plus i have a vacuum gauge (actually a boost guage, hey i can dream can't i?) that reads about 20" of vacuum. same as it always has. the pcv passes the shake test (the only pcv test i know) and the clamps seem tight (non screwing clamp type).<P>another puzzle piece. it's never happened on the x-way. i drove it today and got strait on the x-way (live pretty close) and went strait to the base (only about 5 mls. and again right off the x-way exit). got off and came strait home. no problems. i did let it sit and idle for a few minutes to see what it would do. it kind of stumbled once after a few minutes but kept running. <P>87gtz, once i turned the car off and read the post i went back out but couldn't tell if it was leaking unless it evaporated. whats the best way to check? also i got a new cat as part of an exhaust change (gt exhaust) last spring.<P>i'm going to try to get the permanent fuel pressure guage hooked up to see whats happening while i drive. <P>can you tell me more about the scanner? can i use it for pre dis, or after an engine swap (if i ever pull one off)? i'd want to get all the use out of it i could.<P>thanks for the clues so far. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||04-08-2001||03:36 PM||||Is the plug missing from the base idle screw? If it is, that means someone has been playing with it. If so, the IAC may not be able to control the idle air and it will throw a code. <P>Assuming the idle passages in the TB are clean and the idle hose down to the intake is not leaking (which they love to do), scan the IAC counts. At idle it should be 20-60 counts. <P>If it is higher, then the passages are usually clogged and the IAC is backing out to allow more air to maintain the 900 RPM idle. <P>If they are lower, then there is a leak in the idle path and the IAC is closing completely trying to cut the air off. This could be the throttle plate being misadjusted or a leaking hose.<P>If the plug is gone from the screw, the TB is clean, the IAC pindle is clean and you are sure there are no leaks, scan the IAC counts and adjust the screw to get about 30-40 counts. The idle should settle out at around 900 RPM. <P>Code 35 means the ECM could not maintain 900 RPM +/- 75 for more than 45 seconds. <P>Since it stalls, I'd expect to see the IAC counts very high meaning the idle passages in the TB are clogged (not unusual) and/or the IAC pindle is very dirty.<P>||||reg||1
